<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Merged into the bundle's generated Info.plist by tauri-bundler.

	  tpdf does not use macOS window restoration, and saying so is not only a
	  developer convenience. The app already reopens the document you were
	  reading, on the page you were on, through its own session file
	  (src-tauri/src/session.rs) -- which is the behaviour a reader wants and is
	  ours to get right. Cocoa's restoration would be a second, redundant
	  mechanism layered on top, and the two would disagree the moment they
	  disagreed about anything.

	  What it fixes day to day: an app that terminates abnormally with a window
	  open makes macOS offer "the last time you opened tpdf, it unexpectedly quit
	  while reopening windows -- do you want to try to reopen its windows again?"
	  on the *next* launch, and that dialog blocks the launch until someone
	  clicks it. A development build is killed constantly -- harness timeouts,
	  deliberate crash probes, an aborted panic -- so the prompt arrives in front
	  of a run that has nothing to do with whatever produced it. The same
	  mechanism is how the `RunEvent::Opened` crash in AGENTS.md surfaced a day
	  late, in a dialog, rather than at the time.
	-->
	<key>NSQuitAlwaysKeepsWindows</key>
	<false/>
</dict>
</plist>
